#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116367} :activity/id #uuid "b5ef0f4d-28ef-57bb-a6d8-a44c7baf171f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048233 :topic/id #uuid "8aa7a809-99bd-5db1-b49c-848be170eabe" :topic/math-academy-id 1738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116440 :tutorial/content "Many of the known properties of derivatives of scalar functions extend naturally to derivatives of vector functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions, $u(t)$ is a scalar function, and $\\alpha$ and $\\mathbf c$ are scalar and vector constants, respectively. \n\nThe **sum and multiple rules** for differentiating vector-valued functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)+{g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{sum rule} \\\\ (\\alpha f)^{'}(t)=\\alpha {f}^{'}(t),\\,(uc)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)c\\, & \\text{constant multiple rules} \\\\ (uf)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{scalar function multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10b4a11f-6ada-568e-9a43-dbeeeb1826c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2143 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb0a96a9-97f7-54a8-91fe-e18c9fa61102" :step/math-academy-id 8378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116441 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiating vector-valued functions states that\n\n![](images/57/57f880045af93f552c2736659e377c7ac4c6f41f78a2df446f241783f472f8aa.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcf7f1c428a975daf939f469deb8e13c050d912fb5a2548fbb6b60ea6d413a86.svg) is a vector-valued function while ![](images/cd/cd94c3404a0b6460dd11ffac737b2ce09af9f26b21de28ccedf8d028bd6ec38a.svg) is a scalar function." :tutorial/id #uuid "e299896a-b9ca-574f-8c05-ef656d9c5cd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2144 :tutorial/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "24ff6243-dc48-530e-9e8c-76468e0f51b7" :step/math-academy-id 8379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116442 :tutorial/content "When working with vector functions, there are two types of products: the dot product and the cross product. So, there are two **product rules** for derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{'}(t)=f(t)\\cdot {g}^{'}(t)+{f}^{'}(t)\\cdot g(t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ (f\\times g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Remember that the cross product is anticommutative, meaning that $\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g = - \\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g)(t) = -\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f)(t).\n$$\n\nWhen applying the cross product rule, the order in which the operations are applied is important." :tutorial/id #uuid "babb1734-198a-5751-b78c-b333b6e38d5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2145 :tutorial/title "The Product Rules for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "13df4a33-8393-5b6a-a1df-d349deaa17b2" :step/math-academy-id 8380 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114970 :question/id #uuid "ce098ff9-2ef1-5483-b057-b3fef12af16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2477"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387866}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "4ac8a89c-dc16-5903-a731-4a3fa01f503f" :step/math-academy-id 8381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114971 :question/id #uuid "3668609c-7f98-53ef-8aa7-6f9c3209dfee" :question/math-academy-id "e-2478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393112}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "15461d2a-a63a-5340-8afc-8c7599c24c85" :step/math-academy-id 8382 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114972 :question/id #uuid "cbb15346-028c-59c0-9b20-cd2f408c3bcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393008}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "90197991-2635-5dee-8772-2c779a733d8a" :step/math-academy-id 8383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114973 :question/id #uuid "228ae66f-9dad-5e6d-b405-9fbe62ecb7e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2480"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390798}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "2d325e5d-2cb2-5a4d-9ff0-0babe8095a2f" :step/math-academy-id 8384}] :activity/title "Differentiation Rules for Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115483}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930} :activity/id #uuid "c358bde0-c269-53bb-bce4-8d51f4516e17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185317 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." :tutorial/id #uuid "488db0ee-92f8-5790-8ff6-2977ab0e6dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3187 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "708bbadc-ca98-5148-98dd-f1cc4b9125d0" :step/math-academy-id 653101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184065 :question/id #uuid "2cb88713-f830-501e-9b0a-60320936ad14" :question/math-academy-id "e-4792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442957}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"} :step/id #uuid "0564ce26-4a8b-562e-9ab6-4940e223854b" :step/math-academy-id 653106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184094 :question/id #uuid "132efc65-5f50-5582-8955-a5d34c57af04" :question/math-academy-id "e-8224"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442959}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "ca7e22db-059b-547f-ad06-b3fe2c740ff4" :step/math-academy-id 653103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185345 :tutorial/content "There are many different vector spaces. Some of the most important are listed below.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$, the set of all column vectors of height $n$ with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_n(\\mathbb{R})$, the set of all $n \\times n$ matrices with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the set of all polynomials in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the set of all polynomials of degree less than or equal to $n$ in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The set of all real-valued functions of one variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb7bd53e-69ec-5e14-b970-1bd6ff9aff7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6199 :tutorial/title "Important Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "2b2df015-d32e-5a46-ab00-33e8ccdd4686" :step/math-academy-id 653102 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184420}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008} :activity/id #uuid "71036dcd-e882-527a-9681-dd5cc25371dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54174a8e-4b9f-5398-9c3e-042309cf5af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55b42e-8e25-5d4a-9c6c-fb23853f6637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419001} {:answer/id #uuid "923e4cd4-7cb7-513f-a013-6b459182a21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419002} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a112ad-76a7-5358-b6fe-7be7dc9072ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419003} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa960c-9666-5ef7-ae8f-8608f1b827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} :answer-field/id #uuid "90535267-04a3-5209-88fc-9a5564318779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19147c4b-722c-5ed5-9d91-8a4847b759ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-35626" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f729f32-9ccf-5071-9aab-348325a597e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313cdc82-5d69-5af4-95bb-ace914a012ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43eb35-6698-556b-937a-7540b2eaa194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418997} {:answer/id #uuid "54d7d70f-b06b-5dba-8920-ce4be2335648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418999} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff392f-4de3-5c34-9123-789573d772b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419000} {:answer/id #uuid "f187a6fa-78fb-565f-9a52-c1915abb65f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions  in $A$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628fceb-08d9-5ba5-86ad-00d444e18706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a47334c-0a05-5b9c-b7eb-76cf4c1d5cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94792" :question/problem "Let $A$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form  $f(x)=a+{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because **multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)={x}^{2},$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin A$ because the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in A.$ $\\,✓$"} :